book.chapter(40, "DSLtk.hpp: the reusable REPL")
.endlua
.sh 2 "A shell should not own language semantics"
.pp
The generic dsl::repl::Session template owns interactive mechanics: buffering
physical lines, recognizing directives at form boundaries, history,
completion, prompts, and the last submission outcome. A Language policy
supplies parsing, evaluation, value formatting, and diagnostic formatting.
MetaML's ReplLanguage is one such policy. Another DSL can reuse the shell
without teaching DSLtk about MetaML's constructors, module syntax, or type
inference rules.
.pp
This division is valuable for testing. A language parser can be exercised
without a terminal, and a shell can be tested using a small policy without
the whole MetaML runtime. Terminal-specific features such as Readline and
file-change checks belong in the thin CLI wrapper. The generic session
provides the reusable interaction model, while the application chooses how
physical input and output reach it.
.sh 2 "Incomplete is a distinct state"
.pp
ParseResult distinguishes Ok, Err, and Incomplete. An incomplete result asks
the shell to retain buffered input and request another line. An error
reports a completed failure. At end of input, finish converts a pending
incomplete form into an error rather than waiting forever. Treating an
unterminated string as success with an empty program would lose both the
user's input and the opportunity to provide a useful diagnostic.
.lua
book.graph({
    {id="I", label="Input buffer", x=0, y=0},
    {id="P", label="Policy parse", x=2.3, y=0},
    {id="E", label="Evaluate / report", x=4.6, y=0},
    {id="M", label="Read more", x=2.3, y=-1.2}
}, {{"I", "P"}, {"P", "E", "complete"}, {"P", "M", "incomplete"}, {"M", "I"}},
    "Incomplete input preserves the submission buffer")
.endlua
.pp
The public Outcome adds Quit to Ok, Err, and Incomplete. Feed accepts one
physical line; feed_multiline accepts a chunk; result reports the latest
outcome; pending and buffer expose continuation state. Reset clears the
pending buffer. This shell reset is not necessarily a reset of the language's
bindings or external resources: the policy and registered directives define
those larger operations.
.sh 2 "The language policy is the integration seam"
.lua
book.code("cpp-fragment", [=[// Given a language policy object named language:
dsl::repl::Session shell(language);
shell.feed("first physical line");
if (shell.result() == dsl::repl::Outcome::Incomplete) {
    shell.feed("continuation");
}
shell.finish();]=], "Native shell control flow with an application-provided policy")
.endlua
.pp
The fragment intentionally does not invent a concrete policy: the required
program and value types depend on the language being embedded. Inspect
MetaML's ReplLanguage for a complete integration and the template's submission
routine for the exact callable contract. The D implementation follows the
same separation through its generic Session template and native policy
methods, with module-level rather than C++ namespace-qualified names.
.pp
Keep policy methods deterministic where their output becomes a transcript.
Formatting should not accidentally depend on native map iteration or pointer
addresses. A type query must not execute the expression merely to discover
its type. A trace command deliberately evaluates and should state its
bounded-output policy. Separating these operations helps both interactive
users and automated golden tests understand what a command observes.
.sh 2 "Directives have a boundary"
.pp
The shell recognizes directives at the start of a form when the buffer is
empty. While collecting incomplete input, reset and quit provide escape
routes. Registered directives associate a name, help text, and callback.
MetaML adds language-specific commands such as type, ast, trace, load,
reload, and stack. Their semantics belong to the language policy or wrapper,
not to a hard-coded MetaML branch in the generic toolkit.
.pp
Do not confuse directives with section holes or source comments. A numbered
hash inside an expression belongs to language syntax, while a top-level
named hash can invoke a shell command. The lexer and shell must agree on
where those roles apply. Tests should include multiline input containing
hash-related syntax so directive dispatch does not accidentally consume
ordinary program text in the middle of a form.
.sh 2 "History and completion are host services"
.pp
The generic shell stores history and accepts completion callbacks; the CLI
connects them to terminal libraries and filesystem paths. Completion can
query identifiers, module members, and directive names, but should avoid
executing user code just to propose candidates. A host application may
replace the terminal entirely with an editor widget or network protocol
while retaining the same language-policy boundary.
.pp
MetaML's CLI disables prompts for piped input and offers no-history and
no-tty controls for automation. Its file-change check occurs while processing
later input rather than through a continuously running watcher. These are
wrapper behaviors and should be documented where users choose the executable.
An embedded generic shell does not automatically inherit every filesystem
or terminal policy of the supplied CLI.
.sh 2 "Test transitions, not only final values"
.pp
A REPL regression should cover entering Incomplete, completing the form,
recovering from Err, resetting pending input, and quitting. End-of-file with
an unfinished form deserves its own case. Golden transcripts test prompts,
formatting, and diagnostics; native policy tests can inspect outcomes directly.
The two approaches complement each other because a correct evaluated value
does not prove that buffering or prompt selection behaved correctly.
.sh 2 "Sources and further work"
.pp
Read namespace repl in DSLtk.hpp, the mirrored D subsystem, ReplLanguage in
MetaML.hpp, and metaml-cli/metaml.cpp. Trace one incomplete submission from
feed through parsing to a continuation prompt. Then design a tiny calculator
policy and list which methods it must supply. Explain which changes belong
to that policy, which belong to the generic shell, and which belong only
to a terminal wrapper.
